    <details class="c"><summary><h3>Interview preparation</h3><p>Practice with common interview questions and learn how to answer with confidence.</p><span style="color:var(--blue);font-weight:700;font-size:15px">How to give an interview</span></summary><b class="mod">1. Before the interview</b><ul><li><strong>Know the job:</strong> Read what the job needs and what the company does.</li><li><strong>Keep documents ready:</strong> 2-3 copies of your resume, mark sheets, certificates, ID proof and a passport photo, in one neat folder.</li><li><strong>Dress neatly:</strong> Clean, simple, formal clothes. Neat hair and clean shoes.</li><li><strong>Plan the route:</strong> Reach 10 to 15 minutes early. Never be late.</li><li><strong>Practice aloud:</strong> Practice your answers in front of a mirror or with a friend.</li></ul><b class="mod">2. During the interview</b><ul><li><strong>Start well:</strong> Greet with a smile, say "Good morning, Sir/Madam", and sit only when asked.</li><li><strong>Listen fully:</strong> Let the interviewer finish the question before you answer.</li><li><strong>Speak clearly and honestly:</strong> Short, clear answers. Never lie. If you do not know something, say so and say you are willing to learn.</li><li><strong>Body language:</strong> Sit straight, keep eye contact, and keep your phone on silent in your bag.</li><li><strong>Stay positive:</strong> Never speak badly about your school, teachers or old workplace.</li><li><strong>Ask a question at the end:</strong> For example, "What will my first month at work look like?"</li></ul><b class="mod">3. Common questions and how to answer</b><ul><li><strong>Tell me about yourself.</strong> Give your name, qualification, the ICCE course you did, your main skills, and why you want this job. Keep it under one minute.</li><li><strong>Why do you want this job?</strong> Link the job to your skills. Example: "I have learned MS Excel and typing at ICCE, and this job lets me use them every day."</li><li><strong>What are your strengths?</strong> Pick 2 real strengths, such as quick learning, punctuality or hard work, and give one small example.</li><li><strong>What is your weakness?</strong> Name a small, real weakness and say how you are fixing it. Example: "I get nervous speaking, so I practice daily."</li><li><strong>Why should we hire you?</strong> "I am sincere, I learn fast, and I have the computer skills this job needs."</li><li><strong>What salary do you expect?</strong> As a fresher, say you are open to the company's standard for the role and want to learn and grow.</li><li><strong>Where do you see yourself in 5 years?</strong> Show that you want to grow with the company and improve your skills.</li></ul><b class="mod">4. After the interview</b><ul><li><strong>Say thank you:</strong> Thank the interviewer before you leave.</li><li><strong>Follow up politely:</strong> If you do not hear back in a few days, call or message once, politely.</li><li><strong>Learn from it:</strong> Note what went well and what to improve. Every interview makes the next one easier.</li></ul></details>
    <details class="c"><summary><h3>Communication skills</h3><p>Speak and write confidently at work and in interviews.</p><span style="color:var(--blue);font-weight:700;font-size:15px">How to improve your communication</span></summary><b class="mod">1. Speaking with confidence</b><ul><li><strong>Speak slowly:</strong> Take a breath, speak at a calm speed and keep sentences short.</li><li><strong>Practice daily:</strong> Talk aloud for 10 minutes every day, in front of a mirror or with a friend.</li><li><strong>Record yourself:</strong> Listen to your own voice and fix unclear words.</li><li><strong>Accept mistakes:</strong> Everyone makes mistakes while learning. Keep going.</li></ul><b class="mod">2. Listening and body language</b><ul><li><strong>Listen fully:</strong> Do not interrupt. Let the other person finish.</li><li><strong>Eye contact:</strong> Look at the person you are speaking to, in a natural way.</li><li><strong>Posture and smile:</strong> Sit or stand straight and smile when you greet.</li></ul><b class="mod">3. Useful phrases at work</b><ul><li><strong>Greeting:</strong> "Good morning, Sir/Madam."</li><li><strong>Asking politely:</strong> "May I come in?" and "Could you please repeat that?"</li><li><strong>Promising work:</strong> "I will complete this by 5 PM."</li><li><strong>Closing:</strong> "Thank you for your time."</li></ul><b class="mod">4. Writing messages and emails</b><ul><li><strong>Clear subject:</strong> Write what the email is about in a few words.</li><li><strong>Simple structure:</strong> Greeting, the main point in 2 to 3 lines, then thanks and your name.</li><li><strong>Check before sending:</strong> Read it once to fix spelling mistakes.</li><li><strong>Professional WhatsApp:</strong> Use proper language, no slang, and reply on time at work.</li></ul><b class="mod">5. Phone manners</b><ul><li><strong>Introduce yourself:</strong> "Hello, this is [your name] from [place]."</li><li><strong>Speak clearly:</strong> Choose a quiet place and speak a little slower than normal.</li><li><strong>Take notes:</strong> Write down names, numbers and tasks.</li></ul></details>
    <details class="c"><summary><h3>Job guidance</h3><p>Guidance on where to look for jobs and how to apply.</p><span style="color:var(--blue);font-weight:700;font-size:15px">How to find and get a job</span></summary><b class="mod">1. Decide what you want</b><ul><li><strong>Match your skills:</strong> Choose jobs that fit your ICCE course, such as data entry, computer operator or digital marketing.</li><li><strong>Start small:</strong> Your first job is for experience. It does not have to be perfect.</li></ul><b class="mod">2. Where to look for jobs</b><ul><li><strong>Job websites and apps:</strong> Naukri, Indeed, LinkedIn, Apna and WorkIndia.</li><li><strong>Local places:</strong> Offices, shops, schools, coaching centres, clinics and cyber cafes that need billing, typing and computer work.</li><li><strong>People you know:</strong> Tell friends, relatives and teachers that you are looking for a job.</li><li><strong>Government notices:</strong> Check state and central government job notices regularly.</li></ul><b class="mod">3. How to apply</b><ul><li><strong>Good resume:</strong> Keep it to one page, clear and honest. You can make one in the Resume building card.</li><li><strong>Short message:</strong> Write 3 to 4 lines saying who you are and what job you want.</li><li><strong>Keep a list:</strong> Note every place you applied and the date.</li><li><strong>Follow up:</strong> Contact once, politely, after a few days.</li></ul><b class="mod">4. Keep documents ready</b><ul><li><strong>Originals and copies:</strong> Resume, mark sheets, certificates, ID proof and photos in one folder.</li></ul><b class="mod">5. Stay safe from job scams</b><ul><li><strong>Never pay for a job:</strong> A real employer does not ask for registration or training fees.</li><li><strong>Check the company:</strong> Look for a real office, phone number and website.</li><li><strong>Protect your information:</strong> Do not share OTPs, bank details or ID copies before you are sure.</li><li><strong>Go safely:</strong> Attend interviews at proper offices and tell a family member where you are going.</li></ul><b class="mod">6. In your first job</b><ul><li><strong>Be on time:</strong> Reach early and do not take unnecessary leave.</li><li><strong>Learn and ask:</strong> Ask questions when you do not understand.</li><li><strong>Keep improving:</strong> Learn a new skill every few months.</li></ul></details>
    <details class="c"><summary><h3>Freelancing guidance</h3><p>Start earning online with skills like AI tools and digital marketing.</p><span style="color:var(--blue);font-weight:700;font-size:15px">How to start freelancing</span></summary><b class="mod">1. What is freelancing</b><ul><li><strong>Work for clients online:</strong> You do a task or project for a client and get paid for it, often from home.</li><li><strong>No guarantee:</strong> Earnings depend on your skill, effort and patience. Start small.</li></ul><b class="mod">2. Skills you can start with</b><ul><li><strong>AI tools:</strong> Writing content, making images and short videos with AI.</li><li><strong>Digital marketing:</strong> Social media posts, page management and simple ads.</li><li><strong>Canva design:</strong> Posters, banners, invitations and logos.</li><li><strong>Office work:</strong> Data entry, typing and MS Excel or Word tasks.</li></ul><b class="mod">3. Where to find work</b><ul><li><strong>Freelance websites:</strong> Fiverr, Upwork and Freelancer.</li><li><strong>Local businesses:</strong> Shops, schools, clinics and small businesses that need posters, social media or data work.</li><li><strong>Your own network:</strong> Share your work on Instagram and WhatsApp.</li></ul><b class="mod">4. Getting started</b><ul><li><strong>Make a profile:</strong> Clear photo, a simple headline and the skills you offer.</li><li><strong>Build samples:</strong> Make 3 to 5 sample works to show clients.</li><li><strong>Start with small jobs:</strong> Your first reviews matter more than the first payment.</li><li><strong>Price fairly:</strong> Begin with reasonable rates and raise them as you improve.</li></ul><b class="mod">5. Working with clients</b><ul><li><strong>Understand first:</strong> Ask what the client wants before you start.</li><li><strong>Agree in writing:</strong> Fix the price, work and deadline on chat or email.</li><li><strong>Deliver on time:</strong> Late work loses clients.</li><li><strong>Ask for feedback:</strong> Good reviews bring more work.</li></ul><b class="mod">6. Payments and safety</b><ul><li><strong>Use safe payment:</strong> Use the platform's payment system. For direct clients, take part of the payment in advance.</li><li><strong>Avoid scams:</strong> Never pay to get a freelance job, and be careful if someone asks for your bank details or OTP.</li><li><strong>Keep records:</strong> Note your earnings. Income may be taxable, so ask an accountant if unsure.</li></ul></details>
